## The runtime

This build runs on no engine, so the layer beneath the game is yours as well:
the frame loop and the delta time it hands each update, fitting the fixed
logical stage onto the canvas, the keyboard and the input actions the player
drives the game with, the audio including the cues that loop, loading the
sprites and sounds you produce, and the on-screen debug overlay. The
specification in `specs/` states what Wick needs from that layer and fixes
nothing else about it.
{{else}}
## The {{engine.name}} engine

This build runs on the **{{engine.name}}** engine, installed as a dependency of
the project, so import it by its bare package name; there is nothing to add to
`package.json` for it and nothing to install.

The engine's own documentation is at `{{engine.docs}}`. It is the authoritative
description of the engine's API; the specification in `specs/` describes the
game and does not restate the engine.

Build the game on the engine rather than beside it. The engine owns the frame
loop and the delta time it hands your update, the input actions, the audio
including the cues that loop, asset loading, and the on-screen debug overlay.
It also holds the game's state, by value: each frame it hands `update` the
current state read-only and keeps what `update` returns as the next. What
remains yours is the game itself: its simulation and everything it draws.
{{/if}}

## The specification

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Produce an implementation that matches it exactly.
`specs/overview.md` states what you implement, which files stay as they are,
the code quality the build is held to, the commands that run over the finished
repository, and the build interface. `specs/assets.md` states the sprites,
sheets, icons, and sounds you produce with the asset tools on this machine's
`PATH` and commit with the build.

## Driving the game in a browser

The project's Playwright and its headless Chromium are installed, so the built
game can be served and driven in a real browser. Use the project's own
Playwright: import it from your project (for example
`import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the project-local CLI with
`npx playwright`. There is no global Playwright on `PATH`; it lives in your
project's `node_modules` like any other dependency. Launch Chromium with the
`--no-sandbox` argument, since this container has no Chromium sandbox and a
default launch fails.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a git repository, initialized before you started with the
starter files in its first commit. Your finished implementation is published
directly from this repository's commits, so work you leave uncommitted is not
published. When the build is complete, commit everything: run `git add -A` then
`git commit`, and confirm `git status` reports a clean working tree with nothing
uncommitted or untracked. A `.gitignore` already excludes `node_modules/`, the
build output, and coverage reports, so a clean tree means committing your
source, your tests, your produced sprites and sounds, your
`package-lock.json`, and your `README.md`.
